Files: Getting Data Into Containers

A workload that needs a config file, a calibration table, or a model
has one path: the bytes become an artifact in the local blob store,
and the spec carries only the digest. The journal stays pointers-only
no matter how large the files grow. This document covers the
mechanism, the blocking rule, and how to use it from the CLI, the
SDK, or raw HTTP.

The rule

Every persisted file becomes an artifact. There is no inline content
in specs, no size threshold with two behaviors, no second path. One
mechanism for 200 bytes and 5 gigabytes alike:

  local bytes ──push──▶ blob store via concord-files repo (compat wire)
                              │ syncs peer to peer as chunks, indexed under concord-blobs
  spec ──carries──▶ {path, digest, mode} ──journal──▶ every node
                              │
  assigned node ──pulls──▶ verifies ──writes──▶ container rootfs

Two repos, one blob namespace. File artifacts use the concord-files
repo on the wire (monolithic OCI for compat: HEAD and GET blobs, POST
uploads with digest, PUT and GET manifests). Chunk sync state
uses the concord-blobs repo (one tag per replicated root as the peer
publish index). Serving is repo-independent: the registry answers a
content digest from its blob namespace no matter which repo pushed it,
so one canonical fetch prefix reaches every blob on the peer.

Content addressing does three jobs at once. Dedup: ten workloads
sharing one calibration file store it once; re-pushes upload nothing.
Verification: addressing *is* the checksum, so tampered bytes fail the
pull, never the container. Replication: chunk sync moves only missing
pieces peer to peer with no new control plane.

Pushing: the artifact client

One implementation, shared three ways (sdk/fileart): SDK users call
it directly, the CLI calls it before submit, the daemon calls it at
start. Storage underneath is a chunk store, and the client shape is
unchanged:

- Split. The store splits files into 256KB chunks. The wire stays
  monolithic OCI for compat; chunking lives in the store and sync
  layer transparently.
- Hash. Each chunk hashed with sha256; the root hash is the file
  digest carried in the spec.
- Resume. Store and sync resume at chunk granularity; each
  received chunk verifies against its hash before use.
- Compat. The manifest tag still derives from the blob hex, so sync
  that follows tagged repos keeps working.

  artifacts := fileart.NewClient("127.0.0.1:8444")
  digest, err := artifacts.Push(ctx, content)  // sha256:...; skipped if stored

The store is always the loopback-local one. Bytes never cross the
network except through peer sync.

Blocking: the workload waits

A required blob that has not synced to the assigned node blocks the
workload by design. The mechanism is the fetch error plus retry: a
missing file fails PullTo inside materialize, a missing image layer
fails the pull, the start aborts, and the next tick retries. The
reconciler also nudges the blob syncer (Ensure) with the spec file
digests on a miss as a fast-path optimization over the background
tick; the blocking behavior is unchanged. A digest that never arrives
blocks that workload indefinitely on a partition: starting
half-provisioned is worse than waiting. The debug line per tick says
which digest is missing; concord workload inspect shows the pointers.
Pulls stream to disk with memory flat no matter the blob size: a 5 GB
model never sits in daemon RAM.

Paths and modes

Container paths must be absolute, clean, with no ... Anything else
is a 400 at submit: a relative path could land anywhere, an escape
could climb out of the container root. The materializer re-checks
defensively, because journal lines can be hand-written.

Modes default to 0600 (owner read-write). Pass an explicit mode for
the case that needs it, entrypoint scripts needing the execute bit
being the classic one. Anything above 0777 fails at submit.

  # One flag per file: container path, local path, optional mode
  concord workload run inference:latest --memory 2048 \
    --file /etc/model/config.yaml=./config.yaml \
    --file /entrypoint.sh=./entrypoint.sh:0755

From a Go program

Push first, then reference. The builder stays pure: it carries
pointers, never bytes.

  artifacts := fileart.NewClient("127.0.0.1:8444")
  digest, err := artifacts.Push(ctx, configBytes)
  if err != nil { /* push failed: submit nothing */ }

  w, err := sdk.NewWorkload().
    Image("inference:latest").
    MemoryMB(2048).
    QoS(sdk.QoSGuaranteed).
    File("/etc/model/config.yaml", digest, 0).
    Build()
  id, err := client.Submit(ctx, w)

From raw HTTP: push per the artifact calls above, then submit
the spec JSON with a files array (path, digest, mode). The
endpoint shapes live in docs/ipc.

Size, secrets, garbage: documented, not solved

- Size. No cap. The journal is protected structurally (digests
  only), so store growth is disk planning. The distribution
  doctrine still applies to large models: stage at the depot, pre-warm
  while charging, stagger rollouts (docs/deployment).
- Secrets. Blobs replicate in plaintext to every node. The mesh
  is trusted, so fleet credentials ride fine as-is. Anything with a
  narrower audience than the fleet (customer data, per-tenant keys)
  should be encrypted before the push and decrypted inside the
  container at start: Concord stays a byte store that never sees the
  key, and the application owns its secrecy end to end.
- Garbage. Stopped workloads leave their chunks; dedup softens it,
  cleanup is operator-owned like track rotation and journal archival.
  Store-side collection is a later lever with numbers behind it.

The closed storage list

There are exactly four durable things: the journal (small state,
converges), the kv store (bbolt: views plus sync cursors so restarts
resume mid-history), the blob store (chunks, content-addressed), the
track file (local history). Everything else is ephemeral inside the
container and dies with the workload.

Where it lives

Artifact client (sdk/fileart, shared by SDK, CLI, and daemon),
spec pointers (Files in internal/workload/workload.go, mirrored
in sdk/types.go), submit validation (internal/ipc/handlers.go),
materialization (startContainer in
internal/reconciler/reconciler.go, nudges syncer Ensure on miss, retries
next tick), flags (--file in
internal/cli/workload.go), store (internal/registry binds 0.0.0.0:registry.Port
for peer sync; clients use 127.0.0.1:registry.Port loopback), chunk sync (internal/peersync, started in
internal/runtime/runtime.go before the reconciler, wired via
SetBlobEnsurer). Store shape, wire verbs, caps, and sync details live in
docs/blob.
